{
  "requirement": "Habeas MUST parse Rust, Python, TypeScript, CSS, Markdown, TOML, and YAML\nsources with pinned tree-sitter grammars, selected by file extension compared\ncase-insensitively. A source with any syntax error MUST fail to parse, reporting\nthe line of its first error. Every\nother text file MUST use a plain-text profile in which each line that is not\nblank is an anonymous outline node of kind `line`. Each supported language MUST\nhave an outline profile that maps grammar node kinds to short outline kind names\nand, for each mapped kind, states whether a node is named, and by which fields\nor children, or anonymous. Only mapped kinds MUST appear in the outline; other\nnodes MUST be transparent, so their outline descendants appear under the\nnearest outline ancestor. A profile MAY attach grammar siblings to an outline\nnode, such as Rust attributes and doc comments preceding an item, so the outline\ntree need not match the grammar tree exactly; attached siblings are part of that\nnode for its line range and hash. Every file's outline MUST have a root node\nfor the whole file, independent of language. Source in no other outline node,\nsuch as top-level statements no profile maps, MUST belong to the root node;\nprofiles MUST NOT invent fallback kinds for it. Comments MUST NOT be outline nodes. Profiles MUST be\ndocumented. Upgrading a grammar or profile MAY change outlines and MUST be\ntreated as a deliberate change.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment: the requirement is met.** The implementation carries out each obligation, it is marked {impl}, and tests cover the main points of the design space.\n\n**Implementation**\n- **Pinned grammars:** `Cargo.toml` pins `tree-sitter` and each language crate with `=` versions: css, md, python, rust, toml-ng, typescript and yaml. The Rust, Python, TypeScript/TSX, CSS, Markdown, TOML and YAML profiles each set `language: Some(...)` from their grammar crate.\n- **Profile selection:** `profile_for_path` in `src/outline/profile.rs` lower-cases the extension (`to_ascii_lowercase`) and looks it up in `profiles()`. Anything else, or a path with no extension, gets the plain-text profile.\n- **Syntax errors:** `parse_with` runs `check_errors` after parsing. That function does a pre-order DFS over only the children that have errors, and returns the first `ERROR` or `MISSING` node. The `OutlineError` carries that node's one-based line, and its `Display` form is `line N: ...`. `OutlineCache::parse` in `src/inputs.rs` surfaces the failure, and the `outline` tool turns it into an error.\n- **Plain text:** `plain_text` makes each non-blank line an anonymous `line` node and skips blank lines.\n- **Kind tables and naming:** `KindRule` and `NameRule` (Anonymous, Field, FirstChild, Custom) map grammar kinds to short kind names and say whether and how each node is named. Each language's `KINDS` table is marked {impl}. The custom naming helpers are also marked: CSS `prelude_name` and `selectors_name`, and Markdown `heading_name`. The Rust helpers `impl_name`, `extern_name`, `in_item_list` and `item_macro` are marked {impl} of the more specific `rust_item_selectors` requirement.\n- **Transparency:** `Builder::walk` recurses into unmapped nodes with the same parent, so their mapped descendants appear under the nearest outline ancestor.\n- **Attached siblings:** `Profile::attaches` implements attachment. Rust attributes and outer doc comments attach to the next item, and Python decorators attach to their `def` or `class`. `finish` starts the line range at the earliest attached sibling.\n- **Root node:** `root_node` always builds a root covering the whole file. It is used for both grammar and plain-text files. Unmapped top-level source stays in the root, and no profile has a fallback kind.\n- **Comments:** `maps` excludes anything the profile calls a comment, so comments are never outline nodes. Each profile defines `is_comment`; Markdown uses HTML comment blocks and CSS also counts `js_comment`.\n- **Documentation:** `md/reference.md` (\"Outlines\" and \"Outline profiles\") documents every profile. It gives a table of kind, grammar nodes and name rule for each language, then profile details covering attachment, hashed comments and the YAML/TOML comment placement. It also says grammar versions are pinned exactly in `Cargo.toml` and that upgrading a grammar or profile is a deliberate change. The table matches the `KINDS` tables I read in `rust.rs` and `css.rs`; I did not compare the Python, TypeScript, Markdown, TOML or YAML rows line by line.\n\n**Tests** (`src/outline/tests.rs` and `src/authoring/tools.rs`)\n- `reports_parse_failures_with_lines` checks Rust, Python and TOML errors and that the first error's line is reported (line 3 in the TOML case).\n- `profiles_are_selected_by_extension_case_insensitively` covers upper-case extensions, no extension, and `notes.rs.txt`.\n- The per-language tests cover Python (decorator attachment, root-owned statements), TypeScript/TSX (including `export` transparency), CSS (normalised selector names, at-rules, `@import` unmapped), Markdown (nesting, anonymous leading section, front matter, HTML comment), and TOML/YAML (keys, table arrays, anonymous items, comments before keys).\n- `plain_text_lines_are_interchangeable_anonymous_nodes` covers plain text, including blank lines.\n- `file_level_inner_doc_comments_and_unmapped_code_belong_to_the_root` covers root ownership.\n- `no_profile_invents_fallback_kinds` checks that no profile produces fallback kinds.\n- `outline_lists_nested_nodes_without_text_or_inputs` covers the outline tool end to end, including a missing file and a denied read.\n\nNo FIXMEs or acknowledged gaps were found.",
  "references": "9e663713c98e0a6f4aa8b020065144f361d8dfdce304ffc7fda31fcef8d4348f",
